-------------------------------------------------------------\nClassifying abelian groups through acyclic matchings (https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.08008)\nciteturn0academia12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2.7 years ago; Title: Classifying abelian groups through acyclic matchings\nAuthors: Mohsen Aliabadi, Peter Taylor ... Losonczy to introduce the notion of acyclic matchings in the additive group $\\mathbb{Z}^n$, subsequently extended to abelian groups by the latter author.\nTitle: Classifying abelian groups through acyclic matchings\nAuthors: Mohsen Aliabadi, Peter Taylor\nDate: Mon Feb 12 19:13:16 2024\n\nThe inquiry into identifying sets of monomials that can be eliminated from a generic homogeneous polynomial via a linear change of coordinates was initiated by E. K. Wakeford. This linear algebra problem prompted C. K. Fan and J. Losonczy to introduce the notion of acyclic matchings in the additive group $\\mathbb{Z}^n$, subsequently extended to abelian groups by the latter author. Alon, Fan, Kleitman, and Losonczy established the acyclic matching property for $\\mathbb{Z}^n$. This note aims to classify all abelian groups with respect to the acyclic matching property.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nClassifying Abelian Groups Through Acyclic Matchings (https://d-nb.info/1353735311/34)\nciteturn0search13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2.0 years ago; Classifying Abelian Groups Through Acyclic ... Mohsen Aliabadi\nand Peter Taylor ... matchings in the additive group Zn, subsequently extended to abelian ... Mathematics Subject Classiﬁcation. ... Acyclic matching property, Cyclic group, Torsion-free group.\nAnn. Comb.\nc⃝2024 The Author(s)\nhttps://doi.org/10.1007/s00026-024-00719-w\nAnnals of Combinatorics\nClassifying Abelian Groups Through Acyclic\nMatchings\nMohsen Aliabadi\nand Peter Taylor\nAbstract. The inquiry into identifying sets of monomials that can be elim-\ninated from a generic homogeneous polynomial via a linear change of co-\nordinates was initiated by E. K. Wakeford. This linear algebra problem\nprompted C. K. Fan and J. Losonczy to introduce the notion of acyclic\nmatchings in the additive group Zn, subsequently extended to abelian\ngroups by the latter author. Alon, Fan, Kleitman, and Losonczy estab-\nlished the acyclic matching property for Zn. This note aims to classify all\nabelian groups with respect to the acyclic matching property.\nMathematics Subject Classiﬁcation. Primary 11B75; Secondary 05E16,\n05D15.\nKeywords. Acyclic matching property, Cyclic group, Torsion-free group.\n1. Introduction\nLet (G, +) be an abelian group. Consider nonempty ﬁnite subsets A, B ⊆G.\nA matching from A to B is a bijection f : A →B satisfying the condition\na + f(a) /∈A for all a ∈A. If there exists a matching from A to B, we say A\nis matched to B. Obvious necessary conditions for the existence of a matching\nfrom A to B are |A| = |B| and 0 /∈B. An abelian group G is said to possess\nthe matching property if these conditions on A and B are suﬃcient to ensure\nthe existence of a matching from A to B. The question arises: which groups\nhave the matching property? This question was addressed by Losonczy [11]:\nTheorem 1.1. Let G be an abelian group. Then, G possesses the matching prop-\nerty if and only if G is torsion-free or cyclic of prime order.\nDedicated to Professor Noga Alon on his 69th birthday.\n0123456789().: V,-vol--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nClassifying abelian groups through acyclic matchings - arXiv Combinatorics (https://www.arxiv.symmetricfunctions.com/paper/2402.08008v3)\nciteturn0search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2.7 years ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; ## Classifying abelian groups through acyclic matchings\n\nMohsen Aliabadi, Peter Taylor ... Losonczy to introduce the notion of acyclic matchings in the additive group $\\mathbb{Z}^n$, subsequently extended to abelian groups by the latter author.\n\n## Classifying abelian groups through acyclic matchings\n\nMohsen Aliabadi, Peter Taylor\n\nPublished: 2024-02-12 | Updated: 2024-09-04\n\nComments: Fixed some minor errors. To appear in Annals of Combinatorics\n\n\ngroup matching polynomial\n\n# • BIB • PDF • ARXIV •\n\n### Abstract\n\nThe inquiry into identifying sets of monomials that can be eliminated from a generic homogeneous polynomial via a linear change of coordinates was initiated by E. K. Wakeford. This linear algebra problem prompted C. K. Fan and J. Losonczy to introduce the notion of acyclic matchings in the additive group $\\mathbb{Z}^n$, subsequently extended to abelian groups by the latter author. Alon, Fan, Kleitman, and Losonczy established the acyclic matching property for $\\mathbb{Z}^n$. This note aims to classify all abelian groups with respect to the acyclic matching property.\n\n### BibTeX\n\n[Button: arXiv BibTeX ] [Button: Published BibTeX ]\n\nLoading...\n\n[Button: Copy to Clipboard]\n\nLoading...\n\n[Button: Copy to Clipboard]\n\nMaintained by Per Alexandersson, per@symmetricfunctions.com\n\nBibTeX [Button: ×]\n\n[Button: Copy all]--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSearch Results | Scholarly Articles, Journals, and E-Books | EBSCOhost (https://openurl.ebsco.com/results?bquery=IS+0218-0006+AND+VI+29+AND+IP+4+AND+DT+2025&link_origin=&searchDescription=Annals+of+Combinatorics%2C+2025%2C+Vol+29%2C+Issue+4&sid=ebsco%3Aocu%3Arecord)\nciteturn0search2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 months ago; ### Classifying Abelian Groups Through Acyclic Matchings. ...   * Aliabadi, Mohsen;\n  * Taylor, Peter\n\nPublished in:\n    Annals of Combinatorics, 2025, v. 29, n. 4, p. 979, doi. 10.1007/s00026-025-00745-2\nBy:\n    \n\n  * Kılıç, Yalçın Can\nPublication type:\n    Article\n\n9\n\n### From Hodge Theory for Tame Functions to Ehrhart Theory for Polytopes.\n\nPublished in:\n    Annals of Combinatorics, 2025, v. 29, n. 4, p. 1077, doi. 10.1007/s00026-024-00738-7\nBy:\n   