eld extension.\n  * Matchings and canonical forms for symmetric tensors\n  * Matchings in matroids over abelian groups\n  * On canonical forms.\n  * On matchable subsets in abelian groups and their linear analogues\n  * On matchings in groups\n  * Results and questions on matchings in abelian groups and vector subspaces of fields\n  * Small doubling in groups with moderate torsion\n  * The rectifiability threshold in abelian groups\n\n\nThis page was last edited on 15 June 2026, at 12:01. Warning: Page may not contain recent updates.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nS´eminaire Lotharingien de Combinatoire 91 (2025), Article B91b (https://www.mat.univie.ac.at/~slc/wpapers/s91alia.pdf)\nciteturn0search14 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6 months ago; Every abelian group possesses the weak acyclic matching property. ... Aliabadi, K. ... Taylor. Classifying abelian groups through acyclic matchings.\nEvery abelian group possesses the weak acyclic matching property.\nProof. Let G be an abelian group. Let A and B be nonempty ﬁnite subsets of G\nwith |A| = |B| and A ∩(A + B) = ∅. Since the bijections A →B are precisely\nthe matchings and the group operation is cancellative, we can apply Lemma 2.1 to\nobtain an acyclic matching from A to B, completing the proof.\n□\nRemark 2.4. In the case that A ⊆C and B ⊆C, any operation ⊕: A × B →C\nfor which (C, ⊕) is a quasigroup has the left cancellation property. The deﬁnition\nof matching generalizes straightforwardly to quasigroups, and so does the proof of\nTheorem 2.3. With that in mind, one might explore the properties of matchings in\nbroader contexts beyond abelian groups, such as quasigroups. Delving further into\nthis line of research could prove to be worthwhile.\nReferences\n[1] M. Aliabadi, K. Filom. Results and questions on matchings in abelian groups and vector\nsubspaces of ﬁelds. Journal of Algebra 598 (2022), 85–104.\n[2] M. Aliabadi, M. Janardhanan. On matchable subsets in 