ch the talk\nShi-Mei Ma, On the joint distribution of excedances, fixed points and cycles, Watch the talk\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nClassifying Abelian Groups Through Acyclic Matchings (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/385349095_Classifying_Abelian_Groups_Through_Acyclic_Matchings)\nciteturn0search7 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2.0 years ago; Crawled: 6 months ago; Aliabadi and P. Taylor ... that in an abelian group G if |A|=|B|=|G|−1or|A|=|B|=|G|−2, A must ... the largest possible sets that fail to exhibit acyclic matchings. ... abelian groups to possess the acyclic matching property.\nsum can likewise never be 1. \n\nRemark 2.7. In Lemma 2.6, we come up with two sets A and B of size n−3\n\nthat are not acyclically matched. However, Example 2.2 in [1] demonstrates\n\nthat in an abelian group G if |A|=|B|=|G|−1or|A|=|B|=|G|−2, A must\n\nbe acyclically matched with B. Therefore, A and B in Lemma 2.6 represent\n\nthe largest possible sets that fail to exhibit acyclic matchings.\n\nIn the following theorem, we classify all cyclic groups of prime order\n\nwith respect to the acyclic matching property. This lemma gives a negative\n\nanswer to Question 2.4 in [2], and serves as the “ﬁnite cyclic group” analogue\n\nof Theorem 1 in [5] and Theorem 4.1 in [11].\n\nTheorem 2.8. If p>5 is prime, then Z/p Z does not possess the acyclic match-\n\ning proper ty .\n\nProof. The proof is immediate from Lemma 2.6.\n\n\n\nRemark 2.9. It is worth mentioning that Lemma 2.6 and Theorem 2.8 are\n\napplicable in a more general setting. In essence, we can assert that for n>5,\n\nZ/n Z lacks the acyclic matching property. The case where n is prime follows\n\ndirectly from Theorem 2.8. For non-prime n, choose a such that gcd(a, n)=1.\n\nChoose x∈Z/n Z\\aand set A:= aand B:= (a∪{x})\\{0}. Then,\n\nA is not matched to B, let alone acyclically matched. Therefore, Z/n Z does\n\nnot possess the acyclic matching property. With all this, we are primarily\n\ninterested in the scenario where n is prime, in view of Theorem 1.1.\n\nWe are now ready to provide a necessary and suﬃcient condition for\n\nabelian groups to possess the acyclic matching property. This theorem serves\n\nas the “acyclic matching property” analogue of Theorem 1.1.\n\nContent courtesy of Springer Nature, terms of use apply. Rights reserved.\n\n1024 M. Aliabadi and P. Taylor\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nClassifying Abelian Groups Through Acyclic Matchings (pdf) | Paperity (https://paperity.org/p/358575861/classifying-abelian-groups-through-acyclic-matchings)\nciteturn0search8 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2.1 years ago; Crawled: 3 weeks ago; Aliabadi, Mohsen, Taylor, Peter. Classifying Abelian Groups Through Acyclic Matchings, Annals of Combinatorics, 2024, pp.\nThis is a preview of a remote PDF: https://link.springer.com/content/pdf/10.1007/s00026-024-00719-w.pdf\nArticle home page: https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s00026-024-00719-w\n\nAliabadi, Mohsen, Taylor, Peter. Classifying Abelian Groups Through Acyclic Matchings, Annals of Combinatorics, 2024, pp. 1-7, DOI: 10.1007/s00026-024-00719-w--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSearch Results - \"Taylor, Peter\" :: Library Catalog (https://iris.sena.edu.co/EDS/Search?lookfor=%22Taylor%2C+Peter%22&type=Author)\nciteturn0search9 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 months ago; ## Classifying Abelian Groups Through Acyclic Matchings.\n\nAuthors: Aliabadi, Mohsen Taylor, Peter\n\nAcademic Journal\n\n## Rapid adaptation and extinction in synchronized outdoor evolution experiments of Arabidopsis.\n\nAuthors: Wu, Xing Bellagio, Tatiana Peng, Yunru et al.\n\nSource: Science. 3/26/2026, Vol. 391 Issue 6792, p1-12. 12p.\n\nHTML Full Text PDF Full Text\n\n  2. 2\n\n[Input] [Input]\n\nAcademic Journal\n\n## Why verification and validation of public databases matters: Comment on \"